      id: 'l11', key: 'nc-dq-11', title: 'Regular Expression Matching', concept: 'al-regex-matching', level: 'Hard',
      story: "Implement matching for a pattern with '.' (any single character) and '*' (zero or more of the preceding element), where the whole string must match. Ken Thompson built regex engines into early Unix editors in 1968. Here, dp[i][j] says whether s[i..] matches p[j..], and '*' gives two choices: **skip the starred element** or **consume one character and stay**.",
      analogy: "Checking a ticket against a template where some boxes say 'any letter' and some say 'this letter, any number of times (including none)'.",
      approach: "Top-down: firstMatch = i < |s| and (p[j] === s[i] or p[j] === '.'). If p[j + 1] === '*': match(i, j + 2) or (firstMatch and match(i + 1, j)). Otherwise firstMatch and match(i + 1, j + 1). Memoise on (i, j). Base: j === |p| means match only if i === |s|.",
      brute: ['plain recursion without memo: exponential', "function isMatch(s, p) {\n  if (!p) return !s;\n  const first = s.length > 0 && (p[0] === s[0] || p[0] === '.');\n  if (p[1] === '*') return isMatch(s, p.slice(2)) || (first && isMatch(s.slice(1), p));\n  return first && isMatch(s.slice(1), p.slice(1));\n}"],
      best: ['memoised recursion on indices: O(m · n)', "function isMatch(s, p) {\n  const memo = new Map();\n  const dp = (i, j) => {\n    const key = i * (p.length + 1) + j;\n    if (memo.has(key)) return memo.get(key);\n    let ans;\n    if (j === p.length) ans = i === s.length;\n    else {\n      const first = i < s.length && (p[j] === s[i] || p[j] === '.');\n      ans = p[j + 1] === '*' ? dp(i, j + 2) || (first && dp(i + 1, j)) : first && dp(i + 1, j + 1);\n    }\n    memo.set(key, ans);\n    return ans;\n  };\n  return dp(0, 0);\n}\nconsole.log(isMatch('aa', 'a'), isMatch('aa', 'a*'), isMatch('aab', 'c*a*b'));", 'false true true'],
